                      (    forth6809.asm):03822                                   ; frame correctly). The one combination that
                      (    forth6809.asm):03823                                   ; broke was UNLOOP immediately before EXIT -
                      (    forth6809.asm):03824                                   ; UNLOOP discarding 6 of the 8 bytes itself,
                      (    forth6809.asm):03825                                   ; then EXITUNLOOP unconditionally discarding
                      (    forth6809.asm):03826                                   ; a full 8 more, overshooting into whatever
                      (    forth6809.asm):03827                                   ; sat below (typically the caller's own
                      (    forth6809.asm):03828                                   ; return address) and branching into random
                      (    forth6809.asm):03829                                   ; memory on return. Since UNLOOP has no
                      (    forth6809.asm):03830                                   ; other legitimate use than immediately
                      (    forth6809.asm):03831                                   ; preceding an exit from the definition, and
                      (    forth6809.asm):03832                                   ; EXIT already handles that correctly on its
                      (    forth6809.asm):03833                                   ; own, UNLOOP is now a true no-op rather than
